    "summary": "Portfolio-aware training (Sharpe-tuned hyperparameter selection) provides a statistically significant +0.62 Sharpe improvement over standard per-stock MSE training (p=0.011). LambdaRank shows +0.43 improvement but high variance (not significant, p=0.56). Direct NN Sharpe optimization underperforms baseline, likely due to limited model capacity and noisy gradient surface.",
    "key_finding": "Portfolio-aware hyperparameter selection extracts additional signal from the same noisy OHLCV features, confirming the VLSTM insight that optimizing at the portfolio level is superior to per-stock optimization.",
    "caveats": [
      "5-day forward returns create overlapping daily portfolio returns, inflating absolute Sharpe by ~sqrt(5) \u2248 2.24x. Relative comparisons between approaches are unbiased since all use the same return horizon.",
      "Top-50 stocks by liquidity selected from stocks surviving to 2026 \u2014 survivorship bias may inflate all Sharpe values equally.",
      "Walk-forward validation is not a true out-of-sample test \u2014 the hyperparameter grid was informed by the same feature set.",
      "NN-Sharpe approach (smooth softmax weights) is a poor approximation of hard top-K selection; future work should explore better differentiable sorting operators."
    ],
    "answer_to_research_question": "YES \u2014 portfolio-aware training (via Sharpe-tuned HP selection) does extract more signal from the same noisy features than per-stock MSE training. The improvement is modest (+0.62 Sharpe, +26% relative) but statistically significant. However, this does not overcome the fundamental signal bottleneck: OHLCV-derived features still produce IC \u2248 0.01, far below the ~0.05 needed for reliable live trading."
